select 'The Two Goats on the Bridge', 'Elchin Learning (original)', 'llm', 'fable', 'stubbornness', 'High in the mountains, where the river ran fast and cold, a narrow bridge crossed a deep gorge. The bridge was nothing more than a single wooden plank, worn smooth by many years of hooves. It was so narrow that only one animal could cross at a time, and everyone in the valley knew it. One bright morning, a white goat stepped onto the plank from the eastern bank. At exactly the same moment, a black goat started across from the western side. They met in the middle, with the water roaring far below their feet. "Step back," said the white goat, "because I was already on the bridge before you." The black goat tossed her head and refused to move a single inch. "You step back," she answered, "because I am in a hurry and my side is closer." Neither goat would give way, and the argument grew louder with every word. The white goat lowered his horns, and the black goat lowered hers in reply. They pushed and shoved until the old plank began to tremble beneath them. A moment later, both goats tumbled together into the freezing river. They scrambled to the bank, dripping and ashamed, while the birds laughed from the trees. That evening, two sheep arrived at the same bridge from opposite directions. The first sheep saw the other coming and understood the problem immediately. Without saying a word, she lay down flat on the plank and waited patiently. The second sheep stepped carefully over her woolly back and continued on her way. Then the first sheep rose, shook herself, and crossed to the other side. The goats watched all of this from the bank, still shivering in the evening wind. "We could easily have done that," said the white goat in a quiet voice. The black goat shook the last drops of water from her ears. "Yes," she agreed, "but we were far too busy being right." From that day on, whenever the two goats met on a narrow path, one of them always stepped aside first.', 343, 5.3, 1053, '[{"skill_id":"RD.RL.1","tier":1,"format":"mc4","stem":"Why could only one animal cross the bridge at a time?","options":["The bridge was a single narrow plank.","The river was too loud.","The goats were afraid of the sheep.","The bridge had a gate."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"the noise is mentioned but is not the reason","C":"not in the text","D":"no gate is mentioned"},"explanation":"The text says the bridge was a single wooden plank, so narrow that only one animal could cross.","rule":"RL.5.1 — find the detail the text states directly."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.2","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"What is the theme of this fable?","options":["Being right matters more than being safe.","Giving way a little can save everyone a great deal of trouble.","Goats are stronger than sheep.","Never cross a bridge in the morning."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"the opposite of the lesson","C":"a detail, not a theme","D":"not supported"},"explanation":"The sheep solve the problem by yielding; the goats learn to step aside.","rule":"RL.5.2 — theme from how characters respond to a challenge."},{"skill_id":"RD.V.1","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"\"They scrambled to the bank, dripping and ashamed.\" What does \"ashamed\" mean here?","options":["proud of themselves","embarrassed about what they had done","very cold","angry at the river"],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"opposite","C":"wet, not the same as ashamed","D":"the goats blame themselves, not the river"},"explanation":"They feel bad because their stubbornness made them fall.","rule":"L.5.4a — use the sentence around the word."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.3","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"How are the sheep different from the goats?","options":["The sheep are faster runners.","The sheep solve the problem when one lies down so the other can pass.","The sheep argue for longer.","The sheep swim across the river."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"speed is not mentioned","C":"they say nothing","D":"they cross the bridge"},"explanation":"The sheep cooperate instead of pushing.","rule":"RL.5.3 — compare characters using details."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.6","tier":3,"format":"mc4","stem":"The story is told by a narrator who is not a character. How does this affect what we learn?","options":["We only hear the white goat''s private thoughts.","We see what both goats and the sheep do, from the outside.","We learn the river''s feelings.","We never find out what the sheep did."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"a third-person narrator shows everyone","C":"the river is not a character","D":"the sheep''s actions are shown"},"explanation":"A third-person narrator can show every character''s actions.","rule":"RL.5.6 — point of view shapes what is told."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.1","tier":3,"format":"short_text","stem":"Quote the sentence that shows the goats understood their mistake.","rubric":"Accept: \"We could easily have done that,\" said the white goat in a quiet voice. OR \"but we were far too busy being right.\" OR the final sentence about stepping aside. Must be a quoted sentence from the text that shows the lesson learned.","explanation":"The white goat''s quiet words show he sees what they should have done.","rule":"RL.5.1 — quote accurately."}]'::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from passages where title = 'The Two Goats on the Bridge');
