select 'The Ant Who Saved the Summer', 'Elchin Learning (original)', 'llm', 'fable', 'planning', 'Inside a walled garden on the eastern edge of Baku, an ant named Nara laboured steadily through the hottest weeks of summer. Every morning she carried seeds across the dusty path to the family nest beneath the ancient fig tree. Her cousin Tofiq preferred to recline in the shade of a broad leaf, where he could observe her efforts without lifting a single leg. "The sun is shining and the garden is overflowing with food," he remarked lazily. "Why do you exhaust yourself when you could simply relax?" Nara brushed the dust from her feelers and glanced at the brilliant sky. "The sun will not shine forever," she answered, and returned immediately to her carrying. Tofiq ignored every word of the warning. He devoted his days to chasing drops of honey and entertaining the patient beetles with jokes. Then summer ended, as summers always do, and the weather changed completely. The rains arrived first, and after the rains came the bitter cold. The garden faded to brown, the flowers closed, and every seed along the path disappeared. Tofiq searched through the soaking leaves for hours and discovered nothing whatsoever. His stomach ached and his legs trembled with hunger and exhaustion. Finally he crawled to the fig tree and knocked feebly on the door of Nara''s nest. Nara opened it, and behind her Tofiq glimpsed chambers that were warm and generously stocked with food. He stared at the floor because he could not bring himself to meet her eyes. "You were right," he admitted quietly. "I should have helped you instead of laughing." Nara remained silent for a moment, remembering how exhausted she had been and how loudly he had laughed. Then she stepped aside and held the door open. "Come in," she said, "but when spring arrives, you will carry seeds as well." Tofiq nodded gratefully and stumbled inside. Throughout that winter he learned to count the seeds and mend the walls of the nest. When spring finally returned, he was the first ant through the door, and he carried two seeds at a time.', 345, 6.2, 1200, '[{"skill_id":"RD.RL.1","tier":1,"format":"mc4","stem":"What did Nara do all summer?","options":["She told jokes to the beetles.","She carried seeds to her nest.","She slept under the fig tree.","She chased drops of honey."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"that was Tofiq","C":"not stated","D":"that was Tofiq"},"explanation":"The text says she carried seeds to the nest every morning.","rule":"RL.5.1 — stated detail."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.2","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"Which sentence best states the theme?","options":["Prepare now for harder times later.","Cousins should never argue.","Honey is better than seeds.","Winter is the best season."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"not the lesson","C":"not supported","D":"not supported"},"explanation":"Nara''s planning keeps her safe; Tofiq learns to prepare.","rule":"RL.5.2 — theme."},{"skill_id":"RD.V.1","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"\"He learned to count the seeds and mend the walls of the nest.\" What does \"mend\" mean?","options":["break","paint","repair","count"],"answer":"C","distractor_rationale":{"A":"opposite","B":"not about walls","D":"counting is a separate action in the sentence"},"explanation":"Mending walls means fixing them.","rule":"L.5.4a — context clue: the walls need fixing in winter."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.3","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"How does Tofiq change from the beginning to the end of the story?","options":["He goes from working hard to being lazy.","He goes from laughing at work to doing it first.","He stays exactly the same.","He moves to a different garden."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"backwards","C":"he changes","D":"not in the text"},"explanation":"At the end he is the first ant through the door and carries two seeds.","rule":"RL.5.3 — compare a character across the story."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.4","tier":3,"format":"mc4","stem":"\"The sun will not shine forever.\" What does Nara really mean?","options":["Night is coming soon.","Good times end, so prepare for hard times.","The sun will burn out.","She wants Tofiq to move out of the shade."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"too literal","C":"too literal","D":"not her point"},"explanation":"It is a figurative way of saying summer will end.","rule":"RL.5.4 — figurative meaning."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.1","tier":3,"format":"short_text","stem":"Quote the sentence that shows Nara forgave Tofiq.","rubric":"Accept: Then she stepped aside and held the door open. OR \"Come in,\" she said, \"but when spring arrives, you will carry seeds as well.\" Must quote a sentence showing she lets him in.","explanation":"Stepping aside and saying ''Come in'' shows forgiveness.","rule":"RL.5.1 — quote accurately."}]'::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from passages where title = 'The Ant Who Saved the Summer');
