select 'The Crow and the Water Jar', 'Elchin Learning (original)', 'llm', 'fable', 'problem solving', 'A thirsty crow flew slowly over a parched field on a scorching afternoon in late August. She had not discovered a single drop of water since the early morning. Her throat felt rough, her wings felt heavy, and the ground beneath her shimmered with heat. Eventually she noticed a tall clay jar standing beside an abandoned well. She landed on its rim and peered inside with one bright, curious eye. There was water at the bottom, but only a little, and the jar was tall and extremely narrow. When she pushed her beak inside, it stopped far above the surface of the water. She attempted to tip the jar over with her feet, but it was much too heavy to move. She attempted to crack it with her beak, but the clay was as hard as stone. The crow settled on the rim and considered the problem for a long time. Scattered around the well lay dozens of small grey pebbles. She picked one up, carried it to the jar, and dropped it inside. It fell with a tiny splash, and the water rose by the smallest amount. She dropped another pebble, and then another, watching carefully every time. With every pebble the water climbed a little higher inside the jar. It was slow, exhausting work, and her wings ached from the effort. Nevertheless she did not stop. After forty pebbles the water finally reached the top of the jar. The crow drank until her throat was cool and her head felt clear again. Then she flew to the shade of a fig tree to recover. A young sparrow who had been watching hopped closer along the branch. "How did you know that idea would succeed?" he asked in wonder. The crow closed her eyes against the afternoon sun. "I did not know," she replied. "I only knew that pushing and breaking had failed. So I tried something small, many times over."', 322, 4.8, 961, '[{"skill_id":"RD.RL.1","tier":1,"format":"mc4","stem":"Why couldn''t the crow reach the water at first?","options":["The jar was tall and narrow, and the water was low.","The jar was empty.","The sparrow was in the way.","The well was closed."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"there was a little water","C":"the sparrow only watches","D":"not in the text"},"explanation":"Her beak stopped far above the water because the jar was tall and narrow.","rule":"RL.5.1 — stated detail."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.2","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"What is the message of the story?","options":["Strong birds get what they want.","Small steps, repeated with patience, can solve a hard problem.","Never drink from a jar.","Sparrows ask too many questions."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"force fails in the story","C":"not the point","D":"not the point"},"explanation":"Forty small pebbles did what pushing and breaking could not.","rule":"RL.5.2 — theme."},{"skill_id":"RD.V.1","tier":1,"format":"mc4","stem":"\"She landed on its rim and peered inside with one bright, curious eye.\" What is the \"rim\"?","options":["the bottom of the jar","the top edge of the jar","the water","the well"],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"she looks inside from above","C":"she lands on it","D":"the jar, not the well"},"explanation":"The rim is the edge at the top.","rule":"L.5.4a — context clue."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.5","tier":3,"format":"mc4","stem":"How is the story organised?","options":["A problem, several failed attempts, then a solution.","A list of facts about crows.","A conversation between two birds only.","Events told backwards from the end."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"it is a story","C":"the talk comes at the end","D":"events are in order"},"explanation":"Problem → tipping fails → cracking fails → pebbles work.","rule":"RL.5.5 — how parts of a story fit together."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.6","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"Whose point of view does the narrator mostly follow?","options":["The sparrow''s","The crow''s","The jar''s","The well''s"],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"the sparrow appears only at the end","C":"not a character","D":"not a character"},"explanation":"We follow the crow''s feelings and thoughts.","rule":"RL.5.6 — point of view."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.1","tier":3,"format":"short_text","stem":"Quote the sentence that explains the crow''s strategy in her own words.","rubric":"Accept: \"So I tried something small, many times over.\" (with or without the previous sentence). Must be a quotation, not a paraphrase.","explanation":"That sentence names the strategy: small, repeated tries.","rule":"RL.5.1 — quote accurately."}]'::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from passages where title = 'The Crow and the Water Jar');
