select 'The Shepherd''s Whistle', 'Elchin Learning (original)', 'llm', 'folktale', 'honesty', 'In a village beneath the Caucasus mountains lived a boy named Ilgar, whose family entrusted him with their sheep. Every morning he guided the flock up the green slope above the houses and remained with them until evening. It was quiet, solitary work, and Ilgar was frequently bored. One afternoon, for no reason except boredom, he blew the alarm whistle that hung around his neck. Three sharp notes carried one message to everybody in the valley: a wolf was threatening the flock. The villagers abandoned their tools and hurried up the hill as quickly as they could. They discovered Ilgar sitting on a boulder, laughing until tears ran down his cheeks. "There is no wolf," he announced. "I only wanted to see how fast you could run." The villagers were furious, but they said little and returned to their work. A week later, Ilgar blew the whistle again, and again there was no wolf anywhere. Fewer people climbed the hill that time, and they climbed considerably more slowly. "Do not play games with that whistle," warned old Sona, leaning on her walking stick. "One day you will genuinely need it, and nobody will come." Ilgar merely shrugged and turned back to the sheep. Then, on a grey morning in autumn, a real wolf slipped silently out of the forest. It was thin and hungry, and it moved without disturbing a single leaf. Ilgar blew the whistle with all the breath in his body, three notes and then three more. Down in the village, people glanced up at the hill for a moment. "It is only the boy again," they said, and carried on with their work. Only old Sona collected her stick and began the long, painful climb. By the time she reached the slope, the wolf had seized a lamb and vanished. Ilgar stood trembling beside the frightened flock, unable to speak. Sona rested a hand on his shoulder and waited until he could look at her. "A whistle is only as good as the boy who blows it," she said. Ilgar never blew a false alarm again. It took a long time before the villagers ran when they heard his whistle, but eventually, they did.', 367, 5.8, 1148, '[{"skill_id":"RD.RL.1","tier":1,"format":"mc4","stem":"What did three sharp notes on the whistle mean?","options":["Dinner is ready.","A wolf is threatening the flock.","The sheep are lost.","Come and play."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"not stated","C":"not stated","D":"not stated"},"explanation":"The text says three sharp notes meant a wolf was threatening the flock.","rule":"RL.5.1 — stated detail."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.2","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"What lesson does Ilgar learn?","options":["Sheep are more important than people.","If you lie for fun, people stop believing you when it matters.","Old people are slow.","Wolves only come in autumn."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"not the lesson","C":"Sona is the one who comes","D":"a detail"},"explanation":"His false alarms cost him the villagers'' trust.","rule":"RL.5.2 — theme."},{"skill_id":"RD.V.1","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"\"Ilgar never blew a false alarm again.\" What is a \"false alarm\"?","options":["a broken whistle","a warning about a danger that is not real","a very loud noise","a wolf that runs away"],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"the whistle works","C":"loudness is not the point","D":"not the meaning"},"explanation":"He blew the whistle when there was no wolf.","rule":"L.5.4a — context clue."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.4","tier":3,"format":"mc4","stem":"\"A whistle is only as good as the boy who blows it.\" What does Sona mean?","options":["The whistle needs a new string.","A warning only works if people trust the person giving it.","Boys should not have whistles.","The whistle is broken."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"too literal","C":"not her meaning","D":"too literal"},"explanation":"The whistle''s power depends on Ilgar''s honesty.","rule":"RL.5.4 — figurative language."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.3","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"How is Sona different from the other villagers?","options":["She is the only one who still comes when the whistle sounds.","She is the fastest runner.","She laughs at Ilgar.","She owns the sheep."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"she is old and has a stick","C":"she warns him","D":"not stated"},"explanation":"Only Sona climbs the hill the third time.","rule":"RL.5.3 — compare characters."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.1","tier":3,"format":"short_text","stem":"Quote the sentence that shows the villagers no longer believed Ilgar.","rubric":"Accept: \"It is only the boy again,\" they said, and carried on with their work. Must quote that sentence (or its first part).","explanation":"They dismiss the alarm as the boy''s game.","rule":"RL.5.1 — quote accurately."}]'::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from passages where title = 'The Shepherd''s Whistle');
