select 'The Lantern Race', 'Elchin Learning (original)', 'llm', 'short story', 'friendship', 'Every spring, the children of Qala organised a lantern race along the wide, windswept shore. Each runner carried a paper lantern with a small candle flickering inside it. The winner was the first to reach the ancient stone tower with the flame still burning. Sabina had trained for the race every evening for six weeks without missing a day. She could run the entire length of the beach without pausing for breath. Her friend Kamran was slower, but he was clever with his hands and patient with details. He had constructed his lantern with a tiny window that protected the candle from the wind. The race began just as the sun touched the horizon. Sabina surged ahead immediately, and the sand flew up behind her feet. Halfway to the tower, a sudden gust of wind swept in from the sea. Her candle flickered twice and went out, leaving her lantern completely dark. She stopped and stared at it, unable to believe what had happened. The other runners passed her one by one, their lanterns glowing like miniature moons. Then Kamran arrived, breathing hard, with his own lantern still shining steadily. He looked at her dark lantern, then at the distant tower, and then at her face. Without saying a word, he opened his little window and touched his flame to her wick. Her candle caught and steadied. "Go on," he said. "You are faster than me." Sabina did not move. "We go together," she said. They walked the final stretch side by side, shielding each other''s flames with their hands. They reached the tower last of all the competitors. The judge frowned at them for a moment, and then she laughed aloud. "Two lanterns, both still burning," she said. "I have never seen that in twenty years." She presented them with one small prize to share between them. Sabina still keeps it on the shelf above her bed. She says it is the only race she ever lost and won at the same time.', 334, 4.8, 958, '[{"skill_id":"RD.RL.1","tier":1,"format":"mc4","stem":"How did a runner win the lantern race?","options":["By running the fastest, even with a dark lantern.","By reaching the tower first with the flame still burning.","By building the biggest lantern.","By finishing last."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"the flame had to be lit","C":"size is not mentioned","D":"opposite"},"explanation":"The text states the rule in the third sentence.","rule":"RL.5.1 — stated detail."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.3","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"How are Sabina and Kamran different?","options":["Sabina is fast; Kamran is clever with his hands.","Sabina is clever; Kamran is fast.","They are exactly alike.","Sabina is older."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"swapped","C":"the text contrasts them","D":"not stated"},"explanation":"The second paragraph contrasts their strengths.","rule":"RL.5.3 — compare characters."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.2","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"What is the theme of the story?","options":["Winning is the only thing that matters.","Helping a friend can matter more than winning.","Never run on sand.","Candles are dangerous."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"the opposite","C":"not the point","D":"not the point"},"explanation":"Kamran gives up his chance to win to relight Sabina''s lantern.","rule":"RL.5.2 — theme."},{"skill_id":"RD.V.1","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"\"They walked the final stretch side by side, shielding each other''s flames.\" What does \"shielding\" mean?","options":["blowing out","protecting","lighting","carrying"],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"opposite","C":"already lit","D":"carrying is not the same as protecting"},"explanation":"They protect the flames from the wind.","rule":"L.5.4a — context clue: with their hands, against the wind."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.4","tier":3,"format":"mc4","stem":"\"The only race she ever lost and won at the same time.\" What does this mean?","options":["The judge made a mistake.","She came last but gained something better than a prize.","She ran the race twice.","She won a bigger prize later."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"the judge laughed and gave a prize","C":"one race","D":"not in the text"},"explanation":"She lost the race but won a friendship and a shared prize.","rule":"RL.5.4 — meaning of a phrase."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.1","tier":3,"format":"short_text","stem":"Quote the sentence that shows Kamran chose to help instead of racing on.","rubric":"Accept: Without saying a word, he opened his little window and touched his flame to her wick. Must be that sentence.","explanation":"That sentence shows his choice.","rule":"RL.5.1 — quote accurately."}]'::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from passages where title = 'The Lantern Race');
insert into passages (title, author, origin, genre, topic, text, word_count, fk_grade, lexile_est, questions)
select 'The Girl Who Counted Stars', 'Elchin Learning (original)', 'llm', 'short story', 'perseverance', 'Leyla had decided to count every single star in the sky above her house. Her older brother informed her that this was completely impossible. "There are millions of them," he explained. "You will fall asleep before you reach a hundred." Leyla did not bother to argue with him. She carried a notebook and a pencil up to the flat roof and began. On the first night she counted forty stars and then lost her place among them. On the second night she reached sixty before a thick cloud covered the entire sky. Her brother laughed at her from the window below, and Leyla pretended not to hear. On the third night, she tried something completely different. She held up her fingers and framed a small square of sky between them. Then she counted only the stars inside that square, which turned out to be twelve. She moved the square to the left and counted again, and this time there were nine. She moved it once more and counted eleven. Before long she had filled an entire page with small, careful numbers. In the morning she showed the page to her grandfather over breakfast. He put on his glasses and studied it for a long time without speaking. "You have not counted every star," he observed at last. "No," agreed Leyla, "but I know roughly how many there are. I counted the squares, then multiplied." Her grandfather smiled and set down his tea. "That," he said, "is exactly what astronomers do." That afternoon he took her to the library and found a book with a photograph of the night sky. It showed more stars than Leyla had ever imagined, thousands upon thousands of them. She was not discouraged for a moment. She turned to a clean page in her notebook and drew a new square. Her brother watched from the doorway with his arms folded. After a while he came in, sat down beside her, and asked how the squares worked.', 330, 4.6, 921, '[{"skill_id":"RD.RL.1","tier":1,"format":"mc4","stem":"What went wrong on the second night?","options":["Leyla fell asleep.","A thick cloud covered the sky.","Her notebook was lost.","Her brother took the notebook."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"not stated","C":"not stated","D":"not stated"},"explanation":"The text says a thick cloud covered the entire sky.","rule":"RL.5.1 — stated detail."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.2","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"What is the main message of the story?","options":["Some problems cannot be solved, so give up.","A clever method can make an impossible task possible.","Brothers are always right.","Stars cannot be counted."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"opposite","C":"the brother changes his mind","D":"opposite of the point"},"explanation":"Leyla''s square method turns an impossible count into an estimate.","rule":"RL.5.2 — theme."},{"skill_id":"RD.V.1","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"\"She was not discouraged for a moment.\" What does \"discouraged\" mean?","options":["excited","having lost hope","surprised","tired"],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"opposite","C":"not the meaning","D":"close but not the meaning"},"explanation":"Seeing so many stars could have made her give up, but it did not.","rule":"L.5.4a — context clue."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.3","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"How does Leyla''s brother change during the story?","options":["He starts helpful and becomes unkind.","He starts by laughing and ends by wanting to learn.","He does not change.","He moves to the roof permanently."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"backwards","C":"he changes","D":"not stated"},"explanation":"At the end he sits beside her and asks how the squares work.","rule":"RL.5.3 — character change."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.5","tier":3,"format":"mc4","stem":"Why does the author describe three nights one after another?","options":["To show that each attempt teaches Leyla something and leads to her new method.","To make the story longer.","To describe the weather.","To show the brother was right."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"not a purpose","C":"weather is a detail","D":"he was not"},"explanation":"Each failed night leads to the idea of the square.","rule":"RL.5.5 — how scenes build on one another."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.1","tier":3,"format":"short_text","stem":"Quote the sentence in which Leyla explains her method.","rubric":"Accept: \"I counted the squares, then multiplied.\" (with or without the sentence before it). Must be a quotation.","explanation":"That sentence names the method.","rule":"RL.5.1 — quote accurately."}]'::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from passages where title = 'The Girl Who Counted Stars');
insert into passages (title, author, origin, genre, topic, text, word_count, fk_grade, lexile_est, questions)
select 'The Baker''s Apprentice', 'Elchin Learning (original)', 'llm', 'short story', 'learning from mistakes', 'Rasim had been employed at the bakery on Nizami Street for exactly one week, and so far he had learned only how to sweep the floor and stack the heavy trays. Today, for the very first time, Master Farid was allowing him to prepare the bread himself. "Two cups of water," said Farid, counting slowly on his fingers. "Then the yeast, then the flour, and then you wait patiently." Rasim nodded eagerly, but he was so excited that he poured the flour into the bowl first. Then he added the water, and then he could not find the yeast anywhere. By the time he discovered it behind the sugar, the dough had become a hard grey lump. He kneaded and kneaded until his arms were exhausted, but nothing improved. He placed the lump in the oven anyway and hoped for a miracle. An hour later he pulled out a loaf as flat and heavy as a brick. Rasim''s face burned with embarrassment. He wanted to bury the loaf at the bottom of the bin before Farid could notice it. Instead, he carried it slowly to the counter and set it down. "I did the steps in the wrong order," he confessed. Farid picked up the loaf and knocked on it with his knuckles. It sounded exactly like a stone, and Rasim waited miserably for the shouting to begin. Then Farid did something his apprentice had not expected at all. He laughed, cut a thin slice, and ate it thoughtfully. "Salty," he said, chewing and nodding. "But you told me the truth, and you understand precisely what went wrong. That is worth more to me than one good loaf." He handed Rasim a clean bowl and a fresh packet of yeast. "Again," he said. "Water first this time." The second loaf rose golden and light, and the customers bought every slice before noon. Rasim kept the flat loaf on the shelf above the oven for an entire year. Whenever he felt too sure of himself, he looked up at it and remembered.', 341, 4.9, 990, '[{"skill_id":"RD.RL.1","tier":1,"format":"mc4","stem":"What was the correct order for making the bread?","options":["Flour, water, yeast","Water, yeast, flour","Yeast, flour, water","Flour, yeast, water"],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"Rasim''s wrong order","C":"not stated","D":"not stated"},"explanation":"Farid says: two cups of water, then the yeast, then the flour.","rule":"RL.5.1 — stated detail."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.2","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"What is the theme of the story?","options":["Honesty about a mistake is worth more than hiding it.","Bread must be baked for one hour.","Apprentices should not touch the oven.","Flat bread tastes better."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"a detail","C":"not the lesson","D":"not supported"},"explanation":"Farid values Rasim''s truthfulness more than the loaf.","rule":"RL.5.2 — theme."},{"skill_id":"RD.V.1","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"\"Rasim nodded eagerly, but he was so excited that he poured the flour into the bowl first.\" What does \"eagerly\" mean?","options":["in a keen, impatient way","in a tired way","in a careful way","in a sad way"],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"not the meaning","C":"opposite — eagerness made him careless","D":"not the meaning"},"explanation":"His eagerness made him rush.","rule":"L.5.4a — context clue."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.4","tier":3,"format":"mc4","stem":"\"A loaf as flat and heavy as a brick.\" What kind of language is this, and why is it used?","options":["A simile, to show how badly the bread turned out.","A metaphor, to show the bread was made of clay.","A fact, because bread is a building material.","A joke about houses."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"''as … as'' is a simile, and the bread is not clay","C":"not a fact","D":"not the purpose"},"explanation":"''As flat and heavy as a brick'' compares using ''as''.","rule":"RL.5.4 — simile."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.6","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"From whose point of view do we experience the story?","options":["Master Farid''s","Rasim''s","A customer''s","The oven''s"],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"we do not know Farid''s thoughts","C":"customers appear only at the end","D":"not a character"},"explanation":"We know Rasim''s feelings, such as his burning face.","rule":"RL.5.6 — point of view."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.1","tier":3,"format":"short_text","stem":"Quote the sentence that shows Rasim decided not to hide his mistake.","rubric":"Accept: Instead, he carried it slowly to the counter and set it down. OR \"I did the steps in the wrong order,\" he confessed. Must quote one of these.","explanation":"Carrying the loaf to the counter is the decision.","rule":"RL.5.1 — quote accurately."}]'::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from passages where title = 'The Baker''s Apprentice');
insert into passages (title, author, origin, genre, topic, text, word_count, fk_grade, lexile_est, questions)
select 'The Key in the Fountain', 'Elchin Learning (original)', 'llm', 'adventure', 'curiosity', 'The fountain in the old town square had been dry for as long as anyone could remember. Nigar enjoyed sitting on its stone edge in the afternoons with a library book. One warm Saturday she accidentally dropped her pencil into the empty basin. When she climbed in to retrieve it, she noticed something glinting between two loose stones. It was a small iron key, green with age, and on its handle was a tiny carving of a fish. Nigar carried the key across the square to her uncle, who repaired clocks in the shop opposite the fountain. He turned it over slowly in his careful, experienced hands. "A fish," he murmured. "There was a fish shop on this square before the war. My grandmother used to describe it to me." "Where exactly was it?" asked Nigar, already halfway to the door. He pointed through the window at a bakery with a faded blue door. Nigar went inside and politely asked the baker whether she could examine his cellar. The baker was astonished by the question, but he liked Nigar, and he handed her a lamp. The cellar was cold and smelled of flour and damp stone. In the far wall stood a low wooden door with an iron lock. Nigar''s heart thumped as she fitted the key into the keyhole. It turned with a rusty squeak, and the door swung slowly open. Behind it was a tiny room lined with wooden shelves from floor to ceiling. On the shelves stood rows of glass jars, each with a label written in faded ink. Inside the jars were shells, dried seaweed, and the delicate bones of tiny fish. There was also a notebook, bound in cracked brown leather. The first page said: "Collected from the Caspian shore, 1938." Nigar carried the notebook up into the light of the shop. The baker read it over her shoulder and shook his head in amazement. "A hidden museum," he whispered, "underneath my bread all these years." Today the jars stand in a glass case in the town library, beside a card that records who discovered them.', 350, 5.3, 1048, '[{"skill_id":"RD.RL.1","tier":1,"format":"mc4","stem":"Where did Nigar find the key?","options":["In the bakery cellar","In her uncle''s clock shop","Between two loose stones in the dry fountain","In the library"],"answer":"C","distractor_rationale":{"A":"that is where the door was","B":"she showed it there","D":"where the jars are now"},"explanation":"She saw it glinting between two loose stones in the basin.","rule":"RL.5.1 — stated detail."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.5","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"What clue leads Nigar from the fountain to the bakery?","options":["The pencil","The carving of a fish on the key","The blue door","The lamp"],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"the pencil only leads her into the fountain","C":"the door is where the shop was, not the clue","D":"given later"},"explanation":"The fish carving reminds her uncle of the old fish shop.","rule":"RL.5.5 — how events connect."},{"skill_id":"RD.V.1","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"\"She noticed something glinting between two loose stones.\" What does \"glinting\" mean?","options":["making a noise","flashing with light","growing","melting"],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"you see a glint","C":"not the meaning","D":"not the meaning"},"explanation":"The key catches the light.","rule":"L.5.4a — context clue: she ''noticed'' it."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.2","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"Which idea does the story most support?","options":["Old towns are boring.","Curiosity and asking questions can uncover hidden things.","Keys should be thrown away.","Bakers dislike visitors."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"opposite","C":"opposite","D":"the baker helps"},"explanation":"Nigar''s questions lead to the hidden museum.","rule":"RL.5.2 — theme."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.6","tier":3,"format":"mc4","stem":"The last sentence jumps to ''today''. What does this change of time tell the reader?","options":["That the story was a dream.","That Nigar''s discovery mattered and was shared with everyone.","That the jars were broken.","That the fountain has water again."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"no sign of a dream","C":"they are in a glass case","D":"not stated"},"explanation":"The narrator steps forward in time to show the result.","rule":"RL.5.6 — narrator''s choices shape meaning."},{"skill_id":"RD.RL.1","tier":3,"format":"short_text","stem":"Quote the sentence that tells when the collection was made.","rubric":"Accept: The first page said: \"Collected from the Caspian shore, 1938.\" (or just the quoted label). Must include 1938.","explanation":"The notebook''s first page gives the date.","rule":"RL.5.1 — quote accurately."}]'::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from passages where title = 'The Key in the Fountain');
insert into passages (title, author, origin, genre, topic, text, word_count, fk_grade, lexile_est, questions)
select 'Why the Caspian Is Called a Sea', 'Elchin Learning (original)', 'llm', 'informational', 'geography', 'If you stand on the Boulevard in Baku and look east, you see water stretching all the way to the horizon. Most people call it the Caspian Sea, but geographers argue about that name. A true sea is connected to the world''s oceans, while the Caspian is completely surrounded by land. That makes it the largest enclosed body of water on Earth, bigger than many countries. Five nations share its shoreline: Azerbaijan, Russia, Kazakhstan, Turkmenistan and Iran. So why do we call it a sea and not a lake? The main reason is its size, which is roughly the same as the whole of Japan. Another reason is its water, which is salty, although only about one third as salty as the ocean. Rivers such as the Volga pour fresh water into the northern end, so the north is much less salty than the south. The Caspian is also famous for an ancient fish called the sturgeon. Sturgeon have existed for millions of years and can grow longer than a car. Their eggs, known as caviar, were once so valuable that they were called black gold. Today the sturgeon is endangered, and fishing for it is strictly controlled. The sea has another surprising feature: its level rises and falls over the decades. In the 1930s the water dropped several metres, and old maps show harbours that are now far from the shore. Scientists believe that changes in rainfall and river flow cause these movements. Whether you call it a sea or a lake, the Caspian shapes life in Baku every day. Its winds cool the city in summer, its ports carry oil and grain, and its beaches fill with families every weekend.', 282, 7, 1200, '[{"skill_id":"RD.RI.1","tier":1,"format":"mc4","stem":"According to the passage, why do some geographers say the Caspian is not a true sea?","options":["It is too small.","It is completely surrounded by land.","It has no fish.","It is not salty at all."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"it is huge","C":"sturgeon live there","D":"it is about one third as salty as the ocean"},"explanation":"A true sea connects to the oceans; the Caspian is enclosed by land.","rule":"RI.5.1 — quote or find the stated detail."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.2","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"What is the main idea of the passage?","options":["Sturgeon are the biggest fish in the world.","The Caspian is an unusual body of water that is important to Baku.","Baku has a long boulevard.","Rivers make water fresh."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"one detail","C":"one detail","D":"one detail"},"explanation":"Every paragraph adds a reason the Caspian is unusual and important.","rule":"RI.5.2 — main idea supported by details."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.4","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"In this passage, what does \"enclosed\" mean?","options":["closed in on all sides","very deep","full of ships","warm"],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"not the meaning","C":"not the meaning","D":"not the meaning"},"explanation":"The sentence before says it is completely surrounded by land.","rule":"RI.5.4 — domain vocabulary from context."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.5","tier":3,"format":"mc4","stem":"How does the author organise the part about the sea''s name?","options":["By asking a question and then giving reasons that answer it.","By listing events in the order they happened.","By comparing five countries.","By describing a problem and its solution."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"not chronological","C":"the countries are only named","D":"no problem is solved"},"explanation":"\"So why do we call it a sea?\" is followed by two reasons.","rule":"RI.5.5 — text structure."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.8","tier":3,"format":"mc4","stem":"Which evidence does the author give to support the claim that the sea''s level changes?","options":["Old maps show harbours that are now far from the shore.","The water is salty.","Five nations share the shoreline.","Sturgeon can grow longer than a car."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"supports a different point","C":"supports a different point","D":"supports a different point"},"explanation":"The harbours on old maps are evidence of a lower water level.","rule":"RI.5.8 — reasons and evidence for a point."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.1","tier":3,"format":"short_text","stem":"Quote the sentence that tells how salty the Caspian is compared with the ocean.","rubric":"Accept the sentence: Another reason is its water, which is salty, although only about one third as salty as the ocean. (Partial credit for quoting the phrase ''only about one third as salty as the ocean''.)","explanation":"That sentence compares the saltiness directly.","rule":"RI.5.1 — quote accurately."}]'::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from passages where title = 'Why the Caspian Is Called a Sea');
insert into passages (title, author, origin, genre, topic, text, word_count, fk_grade, lexile_est, questions)
select 'How Bees Make Honey', 'Elchin Learning (original)', 'llm', 'informational', 'life science', 'Honey begins as nectar, the sweet liquid that flowers produce to attract insects. A honeybee lands on a flower and sucks up the nectar with a tongue shaped like a straw. She does not swallow it into her ordinary stomach. Instead, she stores it in a special pouch called the honey stomach, which can hold almost as much as the bee weighs. A single bee may visit more than a hundred flowers before this pouch is full. Back at the hive, the forager passes the nectar to a house bee, mouth to mouth. The house bee holds it for a while and then passes it to another bee. Each time, the bees add substances from their bodies that break the nectar''s sugars into simpler forms. This is why honey is easier to digest than raw nectar. The nectar is then placed into the six-sided wax cells of the honeycomb. At this stage it is still thin and watery, and it would soon spoil. To fix this, hundreds of bees fan their wings above the cells for hours. The moving air evaporates most of the water, until the honey becomes thick and sticky. Finally the bees seal each cell with a thin cap of wax, which keeps the honey clean and dry. A sealed cell of honey can last for many years without going bad. Making honey is enormous work for such small creatures. To produce one kilogram of honey, a hive''s bees fly a distance equal to several times around the Earth. The honey is their winter food, stored when flowers are plentiful and eaten when the fields are bare. Beekeepers take only the extra honey and leave enough for the colony to survive until spring.', 286, 6.1, 1200, '[{"skill_id":"RD.RI.1","tier":1,"format":"mc4","stem":"Where does a forager bee store nectar while flying back to the hive?","options":["In its ordinary stomach","In its honey stomach","In wax cells on its legs","In its wings"],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"the text says she does not","C":"cells are in the hive","D":"not stated"},"explanation":"The passage names the special pouch: the honey stomach.","rule":"RI.5.1 — stated detail."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.5","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"Which structure does the passage mainly use?","options":["Steps in order, from nectar to sealed honey.","A comparison of bees and wasps.","A list of problems with beekeeping.","A story about one bee."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"wasps are not mentioned","C":"not the focus","D":"not a narrative"},"explanation":"The passage follows the process step by step.","rule":"RI.5.5 — chronological / process structure."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.2","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"Which sentence best states a main idea of the passage?","options":["Honey is made through many steps that take a great deal of work.","Flowers are six-sided.","Beekeepers take all the honey.","Bees fly around the Earth."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"cells are six-sided, not flowers","C":"they leave enough for winter","D":"a comparison, not a fact"},"explanation":"The steps and the flying distance both show the work involved.","rule":"RI.5.2 — main idea."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.4","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"\"The moving air evaporates most of the water.\" What does \"evaporates\" mean?","options":["freezes","turns liquid into vapour that leaves","adds sugar","makes it heavier"],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"opposite process","C":"not the meaning","D":"the honey becomes thicker, not heavier"},"explanation":"Fanning removes water as vapour so the honey thickens.","rule":"RI.5.4 — domain vocabulary."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.8","tier":3,"format":"mc4","stem":"Why do the bees fan their wings above the cells? Use the text''s reasoning.","options":["To keep the hive warm.","Because thin, watery nectar would spoil, so the water must be removed.","To attract more bees.","To make the wax caps."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"not the stated reason","C":"not stated","D":"caps come after fanning"},"explanation":"The text says the watery nectar would soon spoil, so they evaporate the water.","rule":"RI.5.8 — reasons that support a point."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.1","tier":3,"format":"short_text","stem":"Quote the sentence that explains what the honey is for.","rubric":"Accept: The honey is their winter food, stored when flowers are plentiful and eaten when the fields are bare. Must quote this sentence or its first clause.","explanation":"That sentence gives the purpose of honey for the colony.","rule":"RI.5.1 — quote accurately."}]'::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from passages where title = 'How Bees Make Honey');
insert into passages (title, author, origin, genre, topic, text, word_count, fk_grade, lexile_est, questions)
select 'Where the Puddle Goes', 'Elchin Learning (original)', 'llm', 'informational', 'physical science', 'After a rain shower, a puddle appears on the playground. By the next afternoon it has vanished, yet nobody swept it away. Where did the water go? The answer is that it turned into a gas called water vapour and floated into the air. This change is called evaporation. It happens because water is made of tiny particles that are far too small to see. The particles are always moving, and the warmer they are, the faster they move. At the surface of the puddle, the fastest particles break free and drift away. Sunshine and wind speed this up, which is why puddles disappear quickly on a hot, breezy day. The water is not destroyed; it is simply spread out in the air as invisible vapour. You can prove the vapour is still there with a cold glass. Fill a glass with ice on a warm day and wait a few minutes. Drops of water soon appear on the outside of the glass. They did not leak through the glass. They came from vapour in the air, which cooled against the cold surface and turned back into liquid. This reverse change is called condensation. Evaporation and condensation are happening all around you, all the time. They dry your washing on the line, fog up the bathroom mirror, and form the clouds above the Caspian. When vapour high in the sky cools, it condenses into droplets that gather into clouds and eventually fall as rain. That rain makes new puddles, and the whole cycle begins again. Nothing is lost in this cycle. The same water particles that filled the playground puddle may once have been part of a cloud, a river, or even a dinosaur''s drink.', 285, 5.7, 1117, '[{"skill_id":"RD.RI.1","tier":1,"format":"mc4","stem":"What happened to the water in the puddle?","options":["It was swept away.","It turned into water vapour and went into the air.","It sank into the ground and disappeared forever.","It froze."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"the text says nobody swept it","C":"not what the text says","D":"not stated"},"explanation":"The passage says the water evaporated into the air.","rule":"RI.5.1 — stated detail."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.4","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"What is \"condensation\" in this passage?","options":["Water vapour turning back into liquid when it cools","Water turning into vapour","Ice melting","Rain falling hard"],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"that is evaporation","C":"melting is different","D":"rain is a result, not the process"},"explanation":"The text calls the reverse change condensation.","rule":"RI.5.4 — domain vocabulary."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.8","tier":3,"format":"mc4","stem":"What evidence does the author use to show that vapour is really in the air?","options":["Drops form on the outside of a cold glass.","Puddles are wet.","Clouds are white.","Dinosaurs drank water."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"not evidence for vapour","C":"not evidence for vapour","D":"an illustration, not evidence"},"explanation":"The cold-glass experiment shows vapour condensing.","rule":"RI.5.8 — evidence for a claim."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.5","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"The passage explains that heat makes evaporation faster. What kind of relationship is this?","options":["Cause and effect","Comparison of two places","A list of dates","A problem with no solution"],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"not a comparison","C":"no dates","D":"not a problem"},"explanation":"Warmth causes particles to move faster, which causes faster evaporation.","rule":"RI.5.5 — cause/effect structure."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.2","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"Which sentence best summarises the passage?","options":["Water moves between liquid and vapour in a cycle, and none of it is lost.","Playgrounds should be covered when it rains.","Cold glasses leak.","Clouds are made of ice only."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"not in the text","C":"the text says they do not","D":"clouds are droplets"},"explanation":"The last paragraph states the cycle idea directly.","rule":"RI.5.2 — summarise."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.1","tier":3,"format":"short_text","stem":"Quote the sentence that says the water in the puddle was not destroyed.","rubric":"Accept: The water is not destroyed; it is simply spread out in the air as invisible vapour. OR Nothing is lost in this cycle. Must quote one of them.","explanation":"Either sentence states that the water still exists.","rule":"RI.5.1 — quote accurately."}]'::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from passages where title = 'Where the Puddle Goes');
insert into passages (title, author, origin, genre, topic, text, word_count, fk_grade, lexile_est, questions)
select 'The Mud Volcanoes of Gobustan', 'Elchin Learning (original)', 'llm', 'informational', 'earth science', 'An hour''s drive from Baku, the land looks like the surface of another planet. Small grey cones rise from the ground, and thick mud bubbles slowly at their tops. These are mud volcanoes, and Azerbaijan has more of them than any other country in the world. Scientists have counted more than three hundred, most of them near Gobustan and along the Caspian coast. A mud volcano is not like a fire volcano. It does not pour out red-hot lava. Instead, it pushes up a cold mixture of mud, water and gas from deep underground. The gas is mostly methane, the same gas that burns in a kitchen stove. Deep beneath the surface, layers of rock press on pockets of gas and water. The pressure builds up until the mixture forces its way through cracks in the ground. Most of the time the volcanoes only bubble and gurgle quietly. Every few years, however, one of them erupts with a roar. Mud shoots dozens of metres into the air, and sometimes the escaping gas catches fire and lights up the night sky. In 2001 a volcano near Baku sent flames hundreds of metres high, and people saw the glow from many kilometres away. Geologists study mud volcanoes because they reveal what lies far below our feet. The mud carries rock and minerals from several kilometres down, which would otherwise be impossible to reach. The volcanoes also show where oil and gas are hidden, since the same pressure that pushes up mud often comes from oil fields. Visitors are welcome to look, but signs warn them not to climb the cones. The mud may look calm, yet the ground beneath it is constantly shifting.', 280, 6.1, 1200, '[{"skill_id":"RD.RI.1","tier":1,"format":"mc4","stem":"How is a mud volcano different from a fire volcano, according to the passage?","options":["It pushes up cold mud, water and gas instead of hot lava.","It is much taller.","It only erupts in winter.","It is found only in the sea."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"the cones are small","C":"not stated","D":"they are on land near Gobustan"},"explanation":"The text says it does not pour out lava but a cold mixture.","rule":"RI.5.1 — stated detail."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.3","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"What causes a mud volcano to erupt?","options":["Rain filling the cone","Pressure building up on gas and water deep underground","Visitors climbing the cones","Cold weather"],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"not stated","C":"not a cause","D":"not stated"},"explanation":"Layers of rock press on gas and water until the mixture forces its way up.","rule":"RI.5.3 — cause and effect between ideas."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.4","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"\"Geologists study mud volcanoes because they reveal what lies far below our feet.\" What does \"reveal\" mean?","options":["hide","show","cook","measure"],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"opposite","C":"not the meaning","D":"not the meaning"},"explanation":"The mud brings up rock from deep down, showing what is there.","rule":"RI.5.4 — vocabulary in context."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.8","tier":3,"format":"mc4","stem":"Which detail best supports the claim that eruptions can be dramatic?","options":["Azerbaijan has more than three hundred mud volcanoes.","In 2001 flames rose hundreds of metres and were seen from far away.","The gas is mostly methane.","Visitors are welcome to look."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"supports a different claim","C":"supports a different claim","D":"supports a different claim"},"explanation":"The 2001 example shows how dramatic an eruption can be.","rule":"RI.5.8 — evidence for a claim."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.2","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"What is the main idea of the last paragraph before the warning?","options":["Mud volcanoes are dangerous to touch.","Scientists value mud volcanoes because they bring up material and clues from deep underground.","Oil is more important than gas.","Gobustan is far from Baku."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"that is the final paragraph","C":"not stated","D":"one hour''s drive"},"explanation":"That paragraph explains why geologists study them.","rule":"RI.5.2 — main idea of a paragraph."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.1","tier":3,"format":"short_text","stem":"Quote the sentence that tells what the gas in a mud volcano mostly is.","rubric":"Accept: The gas is mostly methane, the same gas that burns in a kitchen stove. Must quote this sentence or its first clause.","explanation":"That sentence names methane.","rule":"RI.5.1 — quote accurately."}]'::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from passages where title = 'The Mud Volcanoes of Gobustan');
insert into passages (title, author, origin, genre, topic, text, word_count, fk_grade, lexile_est, questions)
select 'Why a Steel Ship Floats', 'Elchin Learning (original)', 'llm', 'informational', 'physical science', 'Drop a steel spoon into the bath and it sinks at once. Yet a ship made of thousands of tonnes of steel floats across the Caspian every day. How can the same material sink in one shape and float in another? The secret is not the weight of the steel but the space it takes up. Scientists call this idea density, which means how much matter is packed into a certain amount of space. A spoon is solid steel, so it is far denser than water and sinks. A ship, however, is mostly hollow. Its steel walls surround an enormous space full of air, and air is extremely light. When you consider the whole ship, steel and air together, it is less dense than the water around it. That is why it floats. There is a second way to understand this. When a ship sits in the water, it pushes some water aside, or displaces it. The water pushes back with a force called buoyancy. If the weight of the displaced water equals the weight of the ship, the ship floats. A wide, deep hull displaces a great deal of water, so it can carry heavy cargo. This is why ships sit lower in the water when they are fully loaded. Engineers paint a line on the side of every large ship to show the safe limit. If the ship sinks below that line, it is carrying too much. You can test the idea yourself with a ball of modelling clay. Roll it into a ball and it sinks. Flatten the same clay into a bowl shape and it floats, because the bowl takes up more space with the same amount of clay. The clay has not changed, but its density has.', 292, 4.5, 910, '[{"skill_id":"RD.RI.1","tier":1,"format":"mc4","stem":"Why does a steel spoon sink?","options":["It is solid steel and denser than water.","It is too small.","It is painted.","It is hollow."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"size is not the reason","C":"not stated","D":"the ship is hollow, not the spoon"},"explanation":"The passage says a spoon is solid steel and far denser than water.","rule":"RI.5.1 — stated detail."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.4","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"What does \"density\" mean in this passage?","options":["How heavy something is","How much matter is packed into a certain space","How fast something sinks","How big a ship is"],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"weight alone is not density","C":"a result, not the meaning","D":"not the meaning"},"explanation":"The passage defines density directly.","rule":"RI.5.4 — domain vocabulary."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.5","tier":3,"format":"mc4","stem":"How does the author organise the explanation?","options":["By comparing a spoon and a ship, then giving two explanations and an experiment.","By telling the story of one ship.","By listing types of ships.","By describing a shipwreck."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"no story","C":"no list of types","D":"no wreck"},"explanation":"Comparison → density → buoyancy → clay experiment.","rule":"RI.5.5 — text structure."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.8","tier":3,"format":"mc4","stem":"How does the clay experiment support the author''s point?","options":["It shows the same material can sink or float depending on its shape and density.","It shows clay is heavier than steel.","It shows water pushes down.","It shows ships should be made of clay."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"not stated","C":"water pushes back up","D":"not the point"},"explanation":"The clay has not changed, only its density.","rule":"RI.5.8 — evidence supporting a point."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.2","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"Which sentence best states the main idea of the passage?","options":["A ship floats because, taken as a whole, it is less dense than the water it displaces.","Spoons are made of steel.","Ships should be painted with a line.","Clay is heavier than water."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"a detail","C":"a detail","D":"not the point"},"explanation":"Both explanations (density and buoyancy) support this single idea.","rule":"RI.5.2 — main idea supported by details."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.1","tier":3,"format":"short_text","stem":"Quote the sentence that explains what the line painted on a ship''s side shows.","rubric":"Accept: Engineers paint a line on the side of every large ship to show the safe limit. Must quote this sentence.","explanation":"That sentence explains the purpose of the line.","rule":"RI.5.1 — quote accurately."}]'::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from passages where title = 'Why a Steel Ship Floats');
insert into passages (title, author, origin, genre, topic, text, word_count, fk_grade, lexile_est, questions)
select 'Silk and Stone: The Caravanserais of Sheki', 'Elchin Learning (original)', 'llm', 'informational', 'history', 'Long before trains and lorries, goods travelled across Asia on the backs of camels. Merchants moved in groups called caravans, which could include hundreds of animals and people. Their routes, known together as the Silk Road, linked China with Europe. Silk was the most famous cargo, but caravans also carried spices, glass, paper, carpets and ideas. One important stop lay in the green foothills of the Caucasus: the town of Sheki. Sheki had something the merchants wanted very much. Its farmers raised silkworms on mulberry leaves and spun the thread into fine cloth. Traders arrived to buy it and stayed for days, so the town needed somewhere safe for them to sleep. The answer was the caravanserai, a kind of hotel built for caravans. From the outside it looked like a fortress, with thick stone walls and a single gate that closed at night. Inside was a wide courtyard where the camels rested and drank. Around the courtyard ran two floors of small rooms, one for each merchant, with storage for the goods below. Sheki''s two great caravanserais were built in the 1700s and 1800s, and they still stand today. Visitors can walk through the same arched gateway that once welcomed tired traders. A caravanserai was more than a place to sleep. Merchants from many lands ate together, compared prices, and swapped stories. News of new ideas travelled along the Silk Road as surely as the silk itself. When steamships and trains arrived, the camel caravans slowly died out. Yet the caravanserais of Sheki remain, and they remind us that trade has always carried more than goods.', 267, 6.5, 1200, '[{"skill_id":"RD.RI.1","tier":1,"format":"mc4","stem":"What was a caravanserai?","options":["A type of camel","A kind of hotel built for caravans","A silk factory","A railway station"],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"no","C":"silk was made by farmers","D":"railways came later"},"explanation":"The passage defines it directly.","rule":"RI.5.1 — stated detail."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.3","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"Why did Sheki need caravanserais?","options":["Because traders came to buy silk and stayed for days.","Because it rained a lot.","Because the town had no farmers.","Because camels needed hospitals."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"not stated","C":"farmers raised silkworms","D":"not stated"},"explanation":"Traders stayed for days, so the town needed safe lodging.","rule":"RI.5.3 — cause and effect in history."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.4","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"\"Merchants moved in groups called caravans.\" What is a \"merchant\"?","options":["a soldier","a person who buys and sells goods","a camel driver only","a king"],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"no","C":"too narrow","D":"no"},"explanation":"Merchants trade goods.","rule":"RI.5.4 — domain vocabulary."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.2","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"Which sentence best states the main idea of the last paragraph?","options":["Caravanserais were places where ideas and news were exchanged, not just goods.","Steamships were faster than camels.","Sheki is in the Caucasus.","Merchants ate together."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"a detail","C":"a detail from earlier","D":"a detail"},"explanation":"The paragraph ends: trade has always carried more than goods.","rule":"RI.5.2 — main idea."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.5","tier":3,"format":"mc4","stem":"How is the description of the building organised?","options":["From the outside to the inside.","From the newest part to the oldest.","By price of the rooms.","From the roof downwards."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"not by age","C":"no prices","D":"walls and gate first, then courtyard and rooms"},"explanation":"Walls and gate first, then courtyard, then rooms.","rule":"RI.5.5 — structure of a description."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.1","tier":3,"format":"short_text","stem":"Quote the sentence that lists goods other than silk that caravans carried.","rubric":"Accept: Silk was the most famous cargo, but caravans also carried spices, glass, paper, carpets and ideas. Must quote this sentence.","explanation":"That sentence lists the other cargo.","rule":"RI.5.1 — quote accurately."}]'::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from passages where title = 'Silk and Stone: The Caravanserais of Sheki');
