select 'Why Your Brain Needs Sleep', 'Elchin Learning (original)', 'llm', 'informational', 'health', 'Every night you close your eyes and stop noticing the world for eight or nine hours. It looks as if nothing is happening, but your brain is busier than you might think. Scientists who study sleep have discovered that the sleeping brain does several important jobs. The first job is sorting memories. During the day you learn hundreds of things: a new word, a maths method, the way to a friend''s house. While you sleep, the brain replays these experiences and decides which ones to keep. Students who sleep well after studying remember more the next day than students who stay up late. The second job is cleaning. Brain cells produce waste as they work, just as a busy kitchen produces rubbish. During deep sleep, fluid flows through the brain and washes this waste away. Without enough sleep, the waste builds up, and thinking becomes slow and foggy. The third job is growing. The body releases a growth hormone mostly during sleep, which is one reason children need more sleep than adults. Health experts recommend nine to eleven hours a night for children aged six to thirteen. Many children get far less, often because of screens. The light from a phone or tablet tells the brain that it is still daytime, so the brain delays the signal to feel sleepy. Doctors suggest putting screens away an hour before bed and keeping the bedroom dark and cool. Sleep is not wasted time. It is when the day''s learning becomes permanent, when the brain tidies itself, and when the body grows. A good night''s sleep may be the most useful homework of all.', 271, 5.5, 1082, '[{"skill_id":"RD.RI.2","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"What are the three jobs of the sleeping brain described in the passage?","options":["Dreaming, resting and eating","Sorting memories, cleaning and growing","Reading, writing and counting","Watching, listening and talking"],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"not the three named","C":"no","D":"no"},"explanation":"The passage names each job in order.","rule":"RI.5.2 — two or more main ideas."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.1","tier":1,"format":"mc4","stem":"How much sleep do health experts recommend for children aged six to thirteen?","options":["Five to six hours","Nine to eleven hours","Twelve to fourteen hours","Three hours"],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"no","C":"no","D":"no"},"explanation":"The passage states nine to eleven hours.","rule":"RI.5.1 — stated detail."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.8","tier":3,"format":"mc4","stem":"What evidence supports the claim that sleep helps memory?","options":["Students who sleep well after studying remember more than those who stay up late.","Brain cells produce waste.","Phones give off light.","Bedrooms should be cool."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"supports the cleaning job","C":"supports the screens point","D":"advice, not evidence"},"explanation":"The comparison of students is evidence for the memory claim.","rule":"RI.5.8 — evidence for a claim."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.3","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"According to the passage, why do screens make it harder to fall asleep?","options":["Their light tells the brain it is still daytime.","They are heavy.","They make the room hot.","They are noisy."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"no","C":"no","D":"no"},"explanation":"The light delays the sleepy signal.","rule":"RI.5.3 — cause and effect."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.4","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"\"Thinking becomes slow and foggy.\" What does \"foggy\" mean here?","options":["wet","unclear and confused","fast","loud"],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"too literal","C":"opposite","D":"no"},"explanation":"Foggy thinking is unclear, like seeing through fog.","rule":"L.5.4a — figurative use, context clue."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.1","tier":3,"format":"short_text","stem":"Quote the sentence that compares brain waste to something in a kitchen.","rubric":"Accept: Brain cells produce waste as they work, just as a busy kitchen produces rubbish. Must quote this sentence.","explanation":"That sentence contains the kitchen comparison.","rule":"RI.5.1 — quote accurately."}]'::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from passages where title = 'Why Your Brain Needs Sleep');
insert into passages (title, author, origin, genre, topic, text, word_count, fk_grade, lexile_est, questions)
select 'Which Way Is Down?', 'Elchin Learning (original)', 'llm', 'informational', 'physical science', 'Drop a pencil and it falls to the floor. Throw a ball upwards and it always comes back. Something invisible is pulling everything towards the ground, and we call that pull gravity. Gravity is a force between any two objects that have mass. Your body pulls on the Earth, and the Earth pulls on your body. The Earth wins because it is enormously more massive than you are. This is why you feel pulled down, while the Earth barely notices you at all. Here is a question that puzzled people for a long time. If the Earth is round, why don''t people on the other side fall off? The answer is that gravity does not pull towards some floor in space. It pulls towards the centre of the Earth. For a person in Baku, down means towards the centre. For a person in Australia, down also means towards the centre, even though the two people are standing in opposite directions. Everybody on the planet is held on by the same pull towards the same point. Gravity also explains why rain falls, why rivers flow downhill, and why the Moon stays close to the Earth instead of drifting away. Astronauts on the space station look as if they have escaped gravity, but they have not. Gravity there is still almost as strong as on the ground. The astronauts float because they and their station are falling around the Earth together, very fast. You can feel a small version of this when a lift starts to go down and your stomach seems to rise. Scientists measure the pull of gravity carefully. On Earth, a falling object speeds up by almost ten metres per second every second. On the Moon, which has much less mass, the pull is only about one sixth as strong, which is why astronauts there could jump so high.', 310, 5.8, 1140, '[{"skill_id":"RD.RI.1","tier":1,"format":"mc4","stem":"Towards where does gravity pull objects on Earth?","options":["Towards the North Pole","Towards the centre of the Earth","Towards the Sun","Towards the sea"],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"no","C":"no","D":"no"},"explanation":"The passage states this directly.","rule":"RI.5.1 — stated detail."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.5","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"The author asks why people on the other side of the Earth do not fall off. What does this question do in the passage?","options":["It introduces a common misunderstanding and then explains the correct idea.","It changes the subject to the Moon.","It shows the author is unsure.","It lists three facts."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"the Moon comes later","C":"the author answers it","D":"no"},"explanation":"The question sets up the explanation about the centre of the Earth.","rule":"RI.5.5 — problem/question structure."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.8","tier":3,"format":"mc4","stem":"Why, according to the passage, do astronauts float on the space station?","options":["Because there is no gravity in space.","Because they and the station are falling around the Earth together.","Because they are very light.","Because the station is on the Moon."],"answer":"B","distractor_rationale":{"A":"the text says gravity is almost as strong there","C":"no","D":"no"},"explanation":"The passage explains that they are falling together, not that gravity is gone.","rule":"RI.5.8 — the author''s reasoning."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.4","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"\"The Earth wins because it is enormously more massive than you are.\" What does \"massive\" mean here?","options":["having a lot of mass","very old","very round","very far away"],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"no","C":"no","D":"no"},"explanation":"Mass is the amount of matter; the Earth has far more.","rule":"RI.5.4 — domain vocabulary."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.2","tier":2,"format":"mc4","stem":"Which sentence best states the main idea of the whole passage?","options":["Gravity pulls objects towards the centre of the Earth and explains many everyday events.","Astronauts can jump high on the Moon.","Pencils fall to the floor.","Lifts make your stomach rise."],"answer":"A","distractor_rationale":{"B":"a detail","C":"a detail","D":"a detail"},"explanation":"The passage keeps returning to the centre-of-Earth idea and its effects.","rule":"RI.5.2 — main idea."},{"skill_id":"RD.RI.1","tier":3,"format":"short_text","stem":"Quote the sentence that tells how strong the Moon''s gravity is compared with Earth''s.","rubric":"Accept the sentence beginning: On the Moon, which has much less mass, the pull is only about one sixth as strong… Must include ''one sixth''.","explanation":"That sentence gives the comparison.","rule":"RI.5.1 — quote accurately."}]'::jsonb
where not exists (select 1 from passages where title = 'Which Way Is Down?');
